>> title: Chopped Liver
>> category: Dressings & Dips
>> tags: chicken liver, onion, schmaltz, spread, deli
>> counters: Deli
>> aka: chopped chicken liver, gehakte leber, jewish chopped liver, liver spread
>> pairs-with: schmaltz, deli-rye-bread, sour-dill-pickles
>> servings: 8
>> time: 3 hr
>> slack: unforgiving — cooked liver is a two-day item in the fridge whatever the bowl looks like, and the livers go grey and chalky past six minutes a side
>> step.1: boil the eggs 11 min, ice them
>> step.2: fry the onions 25 min in schmaltz
>> step.3: broil the livers 6 min a side
>> step.4: chop coarse with schmaltz
>> step.5: chill 2 hr

Boil @eggs{4%large} in @water{2%qt} for ~boil{11%min}, then cool them under cold water and peel them.

Fry @yellow onions{2%large}(sliced thin) in @schmaltz{3%Tbs} over medium-low heat and ~fry{25%min}, until they are brown and sweet all the way through, not merely soft.

Broil @chicken livers{1%lb}(450 g; trimmed of every green spot and sinew) seasoned with @kosher salt{1%tsp} on a rack close to the element, ~broil{12%min}, turning once, until they are firm and no longer red in the middle.

Chop @&(~1)broiled livers{} together with @&(~2)fried onions{} and @&(~3)two peeled eggs{} in a wooden bowl with a hand chopper, working in @schmaltz{2%Tbs}, @kosher salt{1%tsp} and @black pepper{1/2%tsp} until it holds together but you can still see what it is made of.

Chill @&(~1)chopped liver{} ~chill{2%hr}, then grate the two held-back eggs over the top and serve it with rye and raw onion.
